const articles:Record<string,Article>={
'sip-is-a-method-not-a-product':{category:'MUTUAL FUND BASICS',title:'A SIP is a habit, not an investment product',subtitle:'The monthly instruction is simple. The decision behind it deserves a little more thought.',read:'4 min read',tags:['SIP','NAV','Rupee-cost averaging','Beginner'],intro:'A SIP is often presented as the first step to investing. That is useful shorthand, but it can create a misconception: a SIP is not a fund, a return promise or a risk-control feature. It is a way to invest a chosen amount at regular intervals. The scheme you choose still does the heavy lifting, for better or worse.',sections:[{heading:'Start by separating the two decisions',body:'Think of a mutual fund as the vehicle and a Systematic Investment Plan as the payment route. The fund holds a portfolio of securities according to its mandate. Your SIP instruction tells the platform or fund house to invest a specified amount at a chosen frequency. You can make a SIP into an equity, debt or hybrid scheme. The word SIP tells you how money goes in, not what it is invested in.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Two separate decisions',items:['Choose the investment scheme','Choose how and when to invest'],note:'The scheme determines the portfolio exposure. The SIP determines the contribution schedule.'}},{heading:'What happens on each SIP date?',body:'Your instalment is used to purchase units at the applicable NAV, subject to the transaction cut-off and realisation rules. If the NAV is ₹20 and your instalment is ₹2,000, you receive 100 units before any applicable adjustments. If the next instalment is made when the NAV is ₹16, the same ₹2,000 buys 125 units. When the NAV is ₹25, it buys 80. The number of units changes because the price changes.',visual:{type:'units',title:'Same ₹2,000 instalment, different NAV',items:['₹20 NAV · 100 units','₹16 NAV · 125 units','₹25 NAV · 80 units'],note:'Simplified illustration. Actual allotment depends on applicable NAV and transaction rules.'}},{heading:'Rupee-cost averaging is a mechanism, not a guarantee',body:'Because a fixed amount buys more units when NAV is lower and fewer when it is higher, the average purchase cost per unit can differ from the simple average of the NAVs. This is called rupee-cost averaging. It does not guarantee a profit or protect the investor from a falling market. If the scheme performs poorly over the investment period, a disciplined SIP can still lose money. A SIP also does not guarantee that you will buy at the lowest point. No one knows that point in advance.'},{heading:'SIP versus lump sum is a cash-flow question',body:'If you invest from monthly salary, a SIP may fit the way your income arrives. If you already have a lump sum, the choice is different. Investing it at once exposes the entire amount to the market from that point. Staggering it changes the timing of market exposure, but does not automatically improve the final result. The right comparison depends on your time horizon, liquidity needs, risk tolerance and the source of the money. Avoid treating one method as universally superior.'},{heading:'Before you automate the instruction',body:'Check that the instalment is affordable after essential expenses and emergency savings. Understand the scheme’s objective, risk level, portfolio and costs. Confirm the SIP date, frequency, mandate amount, tenure and cancellation process. Most importantly, know what the money is meant to do. A SIP without a clear purpose can become a standing instruction that continues long after the original goal has changed.'},{heading:"A small example before you automate",body:"Suppose you set a monthly SIP of ₹2,000. Over 12 instalments, you contribute ₹24,000 before considering any returns, costs or missed instalments. That amount does not tell you whether the scheme is appropriate. A debt-oriented scheme and a small-cap equity scheme can both accept SIPs, but their portfolios and possible fluctuations are very different. Before starting, write down the purpose of the money, when you expect to use it and what kind of temporary decline you could withstand. Then assess the scheme on its own merits. The payment schedule should support the investment decision, not replace it."}],takeaways:['A SIP is an investing method, not a mutual fund category.','The NAV on each transaction date determines the units allotted.','Rupee-cost averaging does not assure profit or prevent losses.','Choose the scheme and contribution method as two separate decisions.'],sources:[{label:'Zerodha Varsity: Personal finance and investing',url:'https://zerodha.com/varsity/modules/'},{label:'AMFI: NAV, cut-off timings and applicable NAV rules',url:'https://www.amfiindia.com/investor/knowledge-center-info?zoneName=CutOffTimingsAndNewRuleOnApplicableNAV'},{label:'AMFI Investor: Mutual fund basics',url:'https://www.amfiindia.com/investor'}]},
'compounding-needs-time':{category:'MONEY & TIME',title:'Compounding is not magic. It is time doing the heavy lifting.',subtitle:'A useful mental model for long-term investing, without the fantasy projections.',read:'4 min read',tags:['Compounding','Time horizon','Inflation','Beginner'],intro:'Compounding is one of the most repeated ideas in personal finance. It is also one of the easiest to oversell. The real lesson is not that money grows effortlessly. It is that when returns are earned and retained, they can become part of the base on which future returns are generated. Time gives that process room to work, but it cannot promise the outcome.',sections:[{heading:'The snowball has three moving parts',body:'Imagine a snowball rolling downhill. Its size depends on where it starts, how much snow it picks up and how long it keeps rolling. Investing has similar inputs: the amount already invested, additional contributions and the rate of return earned over time. The analogy has a limit, though. Markets do not roll smoothly downhill. Returns vary, losses happen and the path can be uneven.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'What shapes the compounding journey?',items:['Starting amount','Additional contributions','Returns retained and reinvested','Time in the investment'],note:'Changing any one of these changes the outcome. Returns are uncertain.'}},{heading:'Why time can matter more than a dramatic start',body:'An early start gives contributions and any gains that remain invested more time to participate in future market performance. It also gives an investor more time to build a habit and recover from interruptions. But starting early does not mean taking risks blindly. The investment still needs to match the goal, the time horizon and the investor’s ability to withstand losses.'},{heading:'Nominal growth is not the same as purchasing power',body:'A portfolio value can rise while the cost of living rises too. Inflation reduces what a rupee can buy in the future. When thinking about a long-term goal, it is useful to distinguish the future account balance from its purchasing power in today’s terms. A projection that ignores inflation may make a distant goal look easier to fund than it really is.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'Two different questions',items:['Nominal value: How many rupees might I have?','Real value: What might those rupees buy?'],note:'A goal estimate should consider inflation as well as investment assumptions.'}},{heading:'Why calculator outputs need a warning label',body:'A calculator typically assumes a steady rate of return. Actual market returns do not arrive in a neat straight line. The order of returns, timing of contributions, withdrawals, costs and taxes can all affect the experience. Use calculators to test scenarios, not to forecast a guaranteed corpus. Try a lower-return case, a delayed start and a period when contributions pause. If the plan only works under optimistic assumptions, it needs another look.'},{heading:'The practical takeaway',body:'Build a contribution habit that your finances can support. Increase it when income rises and the budget allows. Keep short-term money out of investments whose value can fluctuate sharply. Review whether the goal amount still reflects its future cost. Compounding rewards time only when the underlying plan is sensible enough to stay with.'},{heading:"How to use a compounding calculator responsibly",body:"Enter a contribution amount and time horizon that reflect your actual cash flow. Then run at least three scenarios: a conservative return assumption, a middle assumption and a more optimistic one. Keep the contribution schedule identical so you can see what the return assumption changes. Next, test a delayed start or a year in which contributions pause. Finally, convert the projected future amount into today’s purchasing power using an inflation assumption. The point is not to find the one correct forecast. It is to discover which assumptions your goal depends on and whether you have room to adjust if reality is less favourable."}],takeaways:['Compounding depends on starting capital, contributions, returns and time.','Market returns are uneven. A smooth calculator line is only an assumption.','Inflation changes the purchasing power of a future corpus.','Use projections to test scenarios, not as promises.'],sources:[{label:'Zerodha Varsity: Personal finance module',url:'https://zerodha.com/varsity/modules/'},{label:'RBI: Inflation and consumer price information',url:'https://www.rbi.org.in/'},{label:'AMFI Investor: Mutual fund risks',url:'https://www.amfiindia.com/investor/knowledge-center-info?zoneName=riskInMutualFunds'}]},
'understanding-mutual-fund-risk':{category:'RISK, WITHOUT THE JARGON',title:'The Riskometer is a starting point. Here is what to look at next.',subtitle:'A label can summarise risk. It cannot tell the whole story of what a portfolio owns.',read:'4 min read',tags:['Riskometer','Credit risk','Market risk','Intermediate'],intro:'The Riskometer is designed to help investors understand the risk level of a mutual fund scheme. It is a useful first check. But a single label cannot explain every source of risk, how the portfolio might react to a particular event or whether the scheme suits your needs. To understand those questions, you have to look a little deeper.',sections:[{heading:'Read the label, then ask what drives it',body:'A scheme’s risk level is assessed using the prescribed framework and disclosed by the fund house. The label can change as the portfolio or its risk characteristics change. It is not a permanent badge and should not be treated as a ranking of expected returns. Two schemes with the same label may hold very different securities and respond differently to market conditions.',visual:{type:'scale',title:'Risk is not one single force',items:['Market movements','Interest-rate changes','Credit quality','Liquidity conditions'],note:'Different schemes carry different combinations of these risks.'}},{heading:'Equity funds: the market is only part of the story',body:'Equity scheme values can move with the broader market, but also with company earnings, valuations, sector cycles, governance events and concentration in a few holdings. A diversified equity fund can still fall sharply during a broad market decline. A sector or thematic fund may have additional concentration because its mandate narrows the opportunity set. Check the portfolio and the scheme objective rather than assuming the category name tells you everything.'},{heading:'Debt funds have risks that can be easy to underestimate',body:'Debt funds are not bank deposits. Interest-rate movements can affect bond prices, particularly when portfolio duration is longer. Credit risk arises when an issuer’s ability or willingness to repay changes. Liquidity risk matters when securities are difficult to sell at a fair price. A debt scheme with a relatively stable NAV over a short period is not automatically free of risk. Read the portfolio, credit quality and maturity or duration information.'},{heading:'A practical factsheet reading sequence',body:'Start with the scheme objective and benchmark. Then inspect the latest portfolio, top holdings, sector or issuer concentration, market-cap mix for equity funds, and credit quality and duration for debt funds. Compare the portfolio across several months to see whether exposures have shifted. Review the Riskometer and benchmark Riskometer together where disclosed. Do not infer safety from a short stretch of calm performance.'},{heading:'Bring the investor into the picture',body:'The same investment can be tolerable for one person and unsuitable for another. Ask when the money is needed, whether a temporary or permanent loss would disrupt the goal, how much liquidity is required and how the investor might react during a decline. Risk capacity is about financial ability. Risk tolerance is about emotional comfort. Both matter. A label cannot make that assessment for you.'},{heading:"Compare two schemes without relying on one label",body:"Imagine two equity schemes both display the same Riskometer level. One may hold a broad portfolio across several sectors, while the other may have higher exposure to a narrow set of industries or companies. The shared label does not make their portfolios interchangeable. Compare their stated mandates, benchmarks, top holdings, sector weights, market-cap mix and changes in exposure over time. For debt schemes, examine credit quality, issuer concentration and duration. Also check the date of each factsheet. A portfolio snapshot is useful evidence, but it is not a guarantee that the holdings will remain unchanged."}],takeaways:['Use the Riskometer as an entry point, not the whole analysis.','Equity and debt schemes carry different types of risk.','Read the latest portfolio and compare exposures over time.','Suitability depends on the investor’s goal, horizon and ability to absorb losses.'],sources:[{label:'AMFI Investor: Risks in mutual funds',url:'https://www.amfiindia.com/investor/knowledge-center-info?zoneName=riskInMutualFunds'},{label:'SEBI: Master Circular for Mutual Funds, March 2026',url:'https://www.sebi.gov.in/legal/master-circulars/mar-2026/master-circular-for-mutual-funds_100491.html'},{label:'Zerodha Varsity: Know your fund',url:'https://zerodha.com/varsity/chapter/know-your-fund/'}]},
'mutual-fund-costs':{category:'THE FINE PRINT',title:'The fee you rarely see can still change your outcome',subtitle:'Expense ratios and exit loads explained, plus the context that a one-line comparison misses.',read:'4 min read',tags:['TER','Expense ratio','Exit load','Intermediate'],intro:'Mutual fund costs rarely arrive as a bill in your inbox. Some are reflected in the scheme’s NAV, others may apply when you transact. That makes them easy to overlook. Understanding the different charges helps you compare like with like, but cost should be one part of a decision, not the only part.',sections:[{heading:'The expense ratio is deducted within the scheme',body:'The Total Expense Ratio, or TER, represents recurring operating expenses charged to a scheme as a percentage of its assets, subject to the applicable regulatory framework. These expenses are reflected in the NAV. Investors generally do not see a separate daily debit from their bank account, which is why the cost can be less visible. AMFI publishes scheme-level TER information, and fund houses disclose it as required.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Where TER sits',items:['Scheme assets','Operating expenses charged','NAV reflects expenses'],note:'TER is a scheme-level recurring cost, not a separate invoice sent to each investor.'}},{heading:'Direct and regular plans are not identical',body:'Direct and regular plans of the same scheme have the same portfolio and fund management, but their expense ratios differ because the distribution expense structure differs. A regular plan includes distribution-related costs and may pay commission to the distributor. A direct plan does not include distributor commission. The choice should account for the service, guidance and transaction support being received, as well as cost. Do not assume that the plan label alone determines suitability.'},{heading:'Exit load is about when you redeem',body:'An exit load may apply if units are redeemed within a specified period. The rate, period and conditions depend on the scheme and may vary by the date and type of transaction. Check the current scheme information document and addenda before redeeming. An exit load is not the same as TER. One is a possible transaction-related charge; the other is a recurring scheme expense.'},{heading:'Compare costs without losing the plot',body:'Compare TER within a relevant category and between plans of the same scheme. Also check whether the funds have similar mandates, portfolio exposures and service arrangements. A lower-cost fund is not automatically the better fit if it has a different objective or risk profile. Equally, a higher fee should not be excused by recent outperformance alone. Costs are more predictable than returns, so they deserve attention, but the full investment case matters.'},{heading:'A three-minute cost check',body:'Find the latest TER on the AMC or AMFI website. Confirm whether you are looking at the direct or regular plan. Read the exit-load table for the units you hold. Check whether any other transaction or statutory charges apply. Finally, compare the cost with funds that genuinely belong to the same category. Save the date of the information because disclosures can change.'},{heading:"A simple way to think about a recurring cost",body:"Assume two otherwise identical investments earn the same gross return before expenses, but one has a higher recurring cost. The difference may look small in a single year. Because expenses are reflected in the NAV, however, the amount left invested is affected repeatedly over the holding period. The exact impact depends on the scheme, changing assets, returns and applicable charges, so avoid treating a one-line calculator as an exact prediction. For a practical comparison, record the current TER for the exact plan and option, review the exit-load schedule and compare schemes with similar mandates. Recheck the figures before investing because disclosures can change."}],takeaways:['TER is a recurring scheme expense reflected in NAV.','Direct and regular plans have different expense structures.','Exit load may apply on redemption under scheme-specific conditions.','Compare costs among genuinely comparable schemes and consider service as well.'],sources:[{label:'AMFI: Current TER of mutual fund schemes',url:'https://www.amfiindia.com/ter-of-mf-schemes'},{label:'SEBI: Mutual Funds Regulations, 2026',url:'https://www.sebi.gov.in/legal/regulations/jul-2026/securities-and-exchange-board-of-india-mutual-funds-regulations-2026-last-amended-on-july-7-2026-_102780.html'},{label:'AMFI: Redemption price and exit load',url:'https://www.amfiindia.com/investor/knowledge-center-info?zoneName=NetAssetValueNAV'},{label:'Zerodha Varsity: Know your fund',url:'https://zerodha.com/varsity/chapter/know-your-fund/'}]},
'portfolio-overlap':{category:'PORTFOLIO INTELLIGENCE',title:'Five funds. One crowded trade? How to spot portfolio overlap.',subtitle:'A portfolio can look diversified on the surface and still depend on the same underlying companies.',read:'4 min read',tags:['Portfolio overlap','Diversification','Concentration','Intermediate'],intro:'There is a familiar portfolio habit: one large-cap fund, another large-cap fund from a different AMC, a flexi-cap fund and perhaps an index fund. The list looks varied. But if several schemes own many of the same companies, the portfolio may be less diversified than the fund count suggests.',sections:[{heading:'A different fund name does not guarantee a different exposure',body:'Portfolio overlap means two or more schemes own some of the same securities. This is common, especially when schemes invest in the same market segment or follow similar benchmarks. The important question is not whether overlap exists, but how much of the combined portfolio depends on the same companies, sectors or risk drivers.',visual:{type:'overlap',title:'Illustrative holdings overlap',items:['Fund A: Alpha · Beta · Gamma','Fund B: Beta · Gamma · Delta','Fund C: Gamma · Delta · Epsilon'],note:'The repeated names are shared holdings. This is a teaching example, not actual fund data.'}},{heading:'Pairwise overlap is only the first pass',body:'Comparing two funds can show their shared holdings, but an investor may own several schemes. A company that appears in three funds can create a meaningful combined exposure even if no single pair looks extreme. Look at the weight of each shared holding, not only the number of names in common. A 1% overlap and a 10% overlap are not the same thing.'},{heading:'Look beyond the top ten holdings',body:'Top holdings are a useful starting point, not a complete portfolio review. Examine sector weights, market-cap exposure, benchmark similarity and the full portfolio where data is available. For debt schemes, issuer concentration, credit quality and maturity profile matter too. Two schemes may own different securities but still be exposed to the same economic risk, such as one sector or interest-rate sensitivity.'},{heading:'Overlap is not automatically a problem',body:'Some overlap is expected and may be intentional. A diversified portfolio does not require every holding to be unique. The question is whether the combined exposures match the investor’s objective and risk capacity. A core index fund and an active fund may overlap substantially by design, while the active fund’s value proposition rests on its differences. Those differences should be understood rather than assumed.'},{heading:'A simple review exercise',body:'Download the latest portfolio disclosures for each scheme and align them to a similar date. Standardise company names and identifiers. Add the weights across the investor’s holdings, accounting for the amount invested in each scheme. Sort by combined exposure to identify the largest shared positions. Repeat at sector level. AMCs also publish category-wise portfolio-overlap disclosures under the updated SEBI framework. Use these as another reference point, while remembering that category-level figures do not replace a review of your own combined holdings. Holdings change, so treat every result as a dated snapshot.'},{heading:"Turn fund overlap into a household-level number",body:"Suppose an investor has ₹5 lakh in Fund A and ₹5 lakh in Fund B. If a company represents 8% of Fund A and 6% of Fund B, the investor’s combined exposure to that company is ₹70,000, or 7% of the ₹10 lakh invested across the two funds. This is more informative than saying the funds have one shared holding. The calculation assumes the two investments are the only holdings being considered and that the portfolio dates align. Extend the exercise across all schemes, then repeat it for sectors. It is a snapshot, not a permanent property of the portfolio."}],takeaways:['Fund count is not a measure of diversification.','Measure shared holdings by combined portfolio weight, not just number of names.','Sector and risk-factor concentration can exist even without identical holdings.','Overlap is a prompt for analysis, not an automatic reason to sell.'],sources:[{label:'Zerodha Varsity: Asset allocation and diversification',url:'https://zerodha.com/varsity/chapter/asset-allocation-an-introduction/'},{label:'Zerodha Varsity: Personal finance review',url:'https://zerodha.com/varsity/chapter/personal-finance-review-part-2/'},{label:'SEBI: Master Circular for Mutual Funds, March 2026',url:'https://www.sebi.gov.in/legal/master-circulars/mar-2026/master-circular-for-mutual-funds_100491.html'},{label:'SEBI: Risk-o-meter and portfolio disclosure circular',url:'https://www.sebi.gov.in/legal/circulars/aug-2021/disclosure-of-risk-o-meter-of-scheme-benchmark-and-portfolio-details-to-the-investors_52262.html'}]},
'review-without-overreacting':{category:'INVESTOR BEHAVIOUR',title:'Your portfolio needs a check-up, not a daily performance review',subtitle:'A useful review asks whether something important changed. It does not ask whether your fund beat its neighbour this week.',read:'4 min read',tags:['Investor behaviour','Portfolio review','Checklist','All levels'],intro:'It is easy to check a portfolio every day. It is harder to know what to do with the information. A red number can trigger anxiety; a green number can create confidence that has not been earned. A review process should help you notice meaningful changes without turning every market move into a decision.',sections:[{heading:'Separate monitoring from decision-making',body:'Monitoring means observing what is happening. Decision-making means changing a contribution, holding or plan. The two do not need to happen together. A market decline may be worth understanding, but it does not automatically mean the original goal or investment case has changed. Equally, a rising market does not prove that a portfolio is well suited to the investor.'},{heading:'Use a calendar, not a headline, to start the review',body:'Choose a review frequency that fits the type of investment and the goal. A long-term portfolio may need a periodic review rather than daily attention. A near-term goal or a material change in income, liabilities or family circumstances may require an earlier check. The point is to have a reason for opening the review, not to react to every news alert.',visual:{type:'timeline',title:'A purposeful review sequence',items:['Goal and time horizon','Personal cash flows and risk capacity','Portfolio exposures and costs','Decision, or no change'],note:'A review can conclude that no action is needed. That is still a decision.'}},{heading:'Four questions to ask before changing anything',body:'Has the goal, amount required or date changed? Has the investor’s ability to absorb losses or need for liquidity changed? Has the scheme’s mandate, portfolio, risk profile or costs changed in a material way? Is the proposed change based on evidence, or mainly on recent performance and emotion? If the answer to the last question is emotion, pause and gather more information.'},{heading:'Do not confuse underperformance with a broken thesis',body:'A scheme can lag its benchmark or peers for a period for many reasons. The review should consider the relevant time horizon, category, benchmark, investment style and portfolio. Look for persistent changes in process, mandate, people, risk or costs rather than relying on one short period. Past performance is not a guarantee of future results, and a review should not become a hunt for last year’s winner.'},{heading:'Keep a short decision log',body:'Record the date, what prompted the review, the facts considered and the decision. If you make a change, write down why. If you decide to stay put, record that too. A simple log helps distinguish a considered process from a string of reactions. It also gives you something to revisit the next time the market becomes noisy.'},{heading:"Separate a review trigger from a market headline",body:"A useful review trigger is a change that could affect the original reason for holding an investment: a goal date moves closer, income becomes less predictable, a scheme changes its mandate, costs change materially or the portfolio develops an exposure you did not intend. A daily price decline, a trending stock-market story or a peer fund’s recent outperformance may deserve attention, but none automatically requires a transaction. Keep a short review note with the date, the evidence checked, what changed and what decision follows. If nothing material has changed, recording that conclusion is still a valid outcome."}],takeaways:['Monitoring does not require a transaction.','Review when a goal, personal circumstance or material investment feature changes.','Compare funds with relevant benchmarks and peers over suitable periods.','Write down the reason for a decision, including a decision to make no change.'],sources:[{label:'Zerodha Varsity: Personal finance review',url:'https://zerodha.com/varsity/chapter/personal-finance-review-part-2/'},{label:'AMFI Investor: Mutual fund risks',url:'https://www.amfiindia.com/investor/knowledge-center-info?zoneName=riskInMutualFunds'},{label:'SEBI Investor: Investor education',url:'https://investor.sebi.gov.in/'}]}
,
'asset-allocation-is-the-real-portfolio':{category:'PORTFOLIO CONSTRUCTION',title:'Asset allocation is the portfolio. Funds are the building blocks.',subtitle:'Why the mix between asset classes often matters more than the number of schemes you own.',read:'4 min read',tags:['Asset allocation','Diversification','Portfolio construction','Intermediate'],intro:'Investors often begin by asking which mutual fund to choose. A more useful first question is how the money should be divided across different kinds of assets. That decision is called asset allocation. It shapes the portfolio’s broad exposure to growth, income, liquidity and market risk. The funds or securities selected within each category come later.',sections:[{heading:'Start with the job each part of the portfolio must do',body:'Different assets behave differently and serve different purposes. Equity can participate in the growth of businesses but can be volatile and suffer large drawdowns. High-quality debt may provide income and a different pattern of risk, but it is exposed to interest rates, credit and liquidity. Cash and cash-like holdings can support near-term needs, although inflation can erode purchasing power. There is no universally correct mix. The appropriate balance depends on the goal, time horizon, cash flows and ability to absorb losses.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'Think in terms of portfolio roles',items:['Growth: long-term participation in business outcomes','Stability: manage volatility and planned liabilities','Liquidity: money available when it is needed'],note:'These are conceptual roles, not a recommended allocation or a fixed asset-class mapping.'}},{heading:'A time horizon is not just a number of years',body:'A goal due in ten years may still require liquidity earlier if contributions are uncertain or the date is inflexible. A goal due in three years may be delayed if the amount is flexible. Consider both the expected date and the consequences of a shortfall. The more essential and near-term the goal, the less room there may be to expose its funding to large market fluctuations.'},{heading:'Diversification works across risk drivers',body:'Owning several schemes does not automatically create diversification. Different labels can hide similar holdings, sectors or sensitivities. Conversely, two assets that look similar on paper may respond differently under some conditions. Think about concentration by issuer, sector, market capitalisation, interest-rate sensitivity, credit quality and liquidity. Diversification can reduce the impact of a single exposure, but it cannot eliminate market-wide losses.'},{heading:'Rebalancing is a discipline, not a market forecast',body:'Over time, assets grow at different rates and the portfolio can drift away from its intended structure. Rebalancing means bringing it back towards a chosen allocation, subject to costs, taxes, liquidity and the investor’s circumstances. It does not require predicting which asset will outperform next. A calendar-based review or a pre-defined tolerance band can make the process more consistent. The method should be documented before markets become emotional.'},{heading:'A practical allocation worksheet',body:'List each goal, the amount required, the target date and how essential the goal is. Record existing assets and liabilities, expected contributions and known cash flows. Separate emergency reserves and near-term commitments from long-term capital. Then consider what level of loss or delay the household could absorb. Only after these questions should an allocation framework and suitable investment categories be explored. This is an educational sequence, not a personalised allocation recommendation.'},{heading:"A practical way to map the role of each asset",body:"List each goal separately and note when the money may be required. Then map the assets intended to fund it, including cash, deposits, bonds, mutual funds and other investments. Ask what job each holding performs: liquidity, income, long-term growth or diversification. Look for unintended dependence on one asset class, issuer, sector or market condition. Two portfolios with the same number of funds can have very different exposures once their underlying assets are combined. This exercise does not produce a universal allocation. It makes the trade-offs visible so the investor can judge whether the mix is consistent with the goal and ability to absorb losses."}],takeaways:['Asset allocation sets the portfolio’s broad risk and return exposures.','Match liquidity and volatility to the purpose and timing of each goal.','Diversification is about different risk drivers, not a large fund count.','Rebalancing should follow a defined process and account for costs and taxes.'],sources:[{label:'SEBI Investor: Investor education and financial planning',url:'https://investor.sebi.gov.in/'},{label:'AMFI Investor: Mutual fund basics and risks',url:'https://www.amfiindia.com/investor'},{label:'Zerodha Varsity: Asset allocation',url:'https://zerodha.com/varsity/chapter/asset-allocation-an-introduction/'}]},
'large-mid-small-cap-are-not-risk-ratings':{category:'UNDERSTANDING CATEGORIES',title:'Large-cap, mid-cap and small-cap are labels. Not risk ratings.',subtitle:'What market-cap categories tell you, what they leave out and why the category name is only the beginning.',read:'4 min read',tags:['Market capitalisation','Equity funds','Risk','Intermediate'],intro:'The words large-cap, mid-cap and small-cap are used as if they describe a complete investment profile. They do not. Market capitalisation is a way to group companies by market value. It can help explain the part of the market a scheme invests in, but it does not tell you whether a particular company is financially strong, fairly valued, liquid or suitable for a specific investor.',sections:[{heading:'Market capitalisation is a measurement, not a quality score',body:'A company’s market capitalisation is generally calculated by multiplying its share price by the number of outstanding shares. Regulatory definitions and category classifications determine how companies are grouped for mutual fund purposes. These classifications can change as market values move and as the applicable framework is updated. Under the current SEBI framework, large-cap companies are ranked 1–100 by full market capitalisation, mid-cap companies 101–250 and small-cap companies 251 onwards. AMFI publishes the classification list and updates it periodically. Check the latest list and scheme documents rather than relying on an old article or a remembered list.',visual:{type:'scale',title:'A category describes relative market size',items:['Large-cap: larger market-value companies','Mid-cap: middle segment by market value','Small-cap: smaller segment by market value'],note:'The exact regulatory classification and list should be checked against current official disclosures.'}},{heading:'Smaller does not automatically mean better growth',body:'Smaller companies may have room to expand, but they can also have narrower businesses, less diversified revenue, lower trading liquidity and greater sensitivity to financing conditions. Larger companies may have established operations and deeper markets, but can still be overvalued, highly leveraged or exposed to disruption. Size alone cannot establish business quality or expected return.'},{heading:'The category affects the journey an investor may experience',body:'Different market-cap segments can behave differently across market cycles. Liquidity, volatility, sector composition and investor flows can vary. A small-cap portfolio may experience sharper price movements and wider gaps between quoted prices and executable prices during stressed conditions. A large-cap portfolio can still fall materially during a broad equity drawdown. No category is a substitute for understanding the underlying holdings.'},{heading:'Read the mandate and the actual portfolio',body:'For a mutual fund scheme, check its stated category, investment objective, benchmark, permitted allocation range and portfolio disclosure. Examine the holdings, sector mix, concentration and market-cap distribution. Compare the scheme with relevant peers and its benchmark over suitable periods. Do not assume that a category name guarantees that every holding will remain in the same market-cap bucket.'},{heading:'Questions to ask before comparing two schemes',body:'Do they belong to the same regulatory category? Do they follow similar investment mandates? Are their benchmarks comparable? How concentrated are their portfolios? What is the liquidity profile? Have their market-cap exposures changed? What are their costs and risks? A comparison becomes more useful when the schemes are genuinely comparable rather than simply sharing a familiar label.'},{heading:"Why the classification list needs a date",body:"A company can move between market-cap buckets as its relative market value changes and as the official list is refreshed. A scheme’s name may remain familiar while its investible universe and actual holdings evolve. When reviewing a fund, note the date of the latest AMFI classification list and the date of the scheme portfolio disclosure. Do not compare a current portfolio with an old classification and assume the labels still line up. The category is a regulatory starting point for understanding the mandate. It is not a promise about future volatility, liquidity, business quality or returns."}],takeaways:['Market capitalisation groups companies by market value, not by quality.','Smaller companies can offer growth opportunities and carry distinct liquidity and volatility risks.','Read the latest category definition, scheme mandate and portfolio.','Compare like with like. Category labels alone are not enough.'],sources:[{label:'SEBI: Categorization and Rationalization of Mutual Fund Schemes, February 2026',url:'https://www.sebi.gov.in/legal/circulars/feb-2026/categorization-and-rationalization-of-mutual-fund-schemes_99983.html'},{label:'AMFI: Categorization of stocks by market capitalisation',url:'https://www.amfiindia.com/research-information/other-data/categorization-of-stocks'}]},
'emergency-fund-before-investing':{category:'FINANCIAL FOUNDATIONS',title:'Before chasing returns, build a buffer for real life.',subtitle:'An emergency reserve is not idle money. It is what can stop an unexpected bill from becoming a forced investment sale.',read:'4 min read',tags:['Emergency fund','Liquidity','Financial planning','Beginner'],intro:'A medical bill, job transition, urgent repair or delayed payment can arrive at the wrong time. If every rupee is invested in a market-linked product, an unexpected expense may force a sale when prices are unfavourable. An emergency reserve is designed for access and resilience, not for maximising returns.',sections:[{heading:'The purpose is to protect choices',body:'An emergency reserve gives a household time to respond to an unexpected expense without immediately borrowing at a high cost or liquidating long-term investments. It can protect an investment plan from being interrupted by events that have nothing to do with market conditions. The reserve is not meant to outperform equity or beat inflation. Its value lies in availability when needed.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'What a cash-flow shock can trigger',items:['Unexpected expense or income interruption','Available reserve absorbs the immediate need','Long-term investments are less likely to be sold under pressure'],note:'Illustrative sequence. Actual needs depend on household circumstances.'}},{heading:'Size the buffer around your own obligations',body:'A common starting exercise is to estimate essential monthly outgoings, then consider how many months of those expenses might need to be covered. The answer depends on income stability, number of earners, dependants, insurance coverage, debt obligations, job mobility and access to other reliable resources. A household with variable income or a single primary earner may need a different buffer from one with stable dual incomes.'},{heading:'Liquidity and capital stability come before yield',body:'Emergency money should be accessible within the timeframe in which a genuine emergency could arise. Understand withdrawal restrictions, settlement time, penalties, credit risk and the possibility of value fluctuation. A product with a higher advertised return may not be appropriate if access is delayed or principal value can move materially. Do not assume every product described as liquid is equivalent to a bank savings balance.'},{heading:'Insurance and an emergency fund do different jobs',body:'Insurance can transfer specified financial risks subject to policy terms, exclusions, limits and claim conditions. An emergency reserve can meet costs that are not covered, pay deductibles or bridge timing gaps. Neither replaces the other. Review health and life protection needs in the context of dependants, liabilities and existing coverage, and seek qualified insurance or tax guidance where needed.'},{heading:'Build it in stages and keep it separate',body:'If the full reserve feels difficult to create at once, set an initial milestone and build it gradually from cash flow. Keep the reserve identifiable and avoid treating it as money available for discretionary purchases or market opportunities. Replenish it after use and revisit the target when household expenses, income or responsibilities change. The aim is a buffer that is realistic enough to maintain.'},{heading:"Put a number around the buffer",body:"Start with essential monthly expenses rather than total lifestyle spending. Include housing, groceries, utilities, insurance premiums, transport, minimum debt payments and unavoidable family commitments. If essential outgoings are ₹40,000 a month, a six-month reference buffer would be ₹2.4 lakh. That is an illustration, not a universal rule. A variable income, single-income household, dependent family members or limited access to credit may justify a larger reserve. Keep the money accessible and consider the risks of the account or instrument used. Review the amount when expenses or responsibilities change, and do not count volatile investments as cash available for an emergency."}],takeaways:['An emergency reserve protects liquidity and reduces the chance of forced selling.','Estimate essential expenses and consider income stability, dependants and liabilities.','Prioritise access and capital stability over chasing yield.','Insurance and emergency savings solve different parts of the risk problem.'],sources:[{label:'RBI: Financial education resources',url:'https://www.rbi.org.in/FinancialEducation/'},{label:'SEBI Investor: Financial planning resources',url:'https://investor.sebi.gov.in/'},{label:'IRDAI: Consumer education and policyholder resources',url:'https://irdai.gov.in/'}]},
'market-cycles-and-changing-leadership':{category:'MARKET CONTEXT',title:'Markets move in cycles. Leadership rarely stays in one place.',subtitle:'Why sectors and styles take turns, and why yesterday’s winner is a poor portfolio plan by itself.',read:'4 min read',tags:['Market cycles','Sector rotation','Behaviour','Intermediate'],intro:'Market leadership changes. A sector that dominates one period may lag in another as earnings expectations, interest rates, valuations, policy, liquidity and investor positioning evolve. Recognising that leadership can rotate is useful. Trying to predict every rotation is a different and much harder task.',sections:[{heading:'A market cycle is a changing set of conditions',body:'Economic growth, inflation, interest rates, credit availability, corporate earnings and investor risk appetite interact over time. These forces do not move in a neat sequence, and markets often anticipate changes before they appear in published economic data. A cycle framework is therefore a way to organise observations, not a reliable calendar that tells investors what happens next.',visual:{type:'timeline',title:'Conditions that can influence leadership',items:['Growth and earnings expectations','Inflation and interest-rate direction','Liquidity and credit conditions','Valuations and investor positioning'],note:'These forces interact. No single indicator defines a market cycle.'}},{heading:'Why leadership rotates',body:'Different businesses respond differently to the same environment. Rate-sensitive companies may react to changing borrowing costs. Commodity producers can be affected by input prices and global demand. Defensive businesses may attract attention when uncertainty rises, while cyclical businesses may lead when growth expectations improve. These are tendencies, not rules. Company-specific execution and valuations can overwhelm a broad sector narrative.'},{heading:'Index performance can hide a narrow market',body:'A headline index can rise while many constituents lag, or fall while a smaller group of large companies holds it up. Breadth measures, equal-weight comparisons and sector participation can add context. They help distinguish a broad advance from one led by a narrow set of stocks. Breadth is still a descriptive measure and can change quickly; it does not independently predict the next move.'},{heading:'Do not turn rotation into performance chasing',body:'Moving into the latest outperforming sector after a strong run can mean buying after expectations have already risen. The sector may continue to lead, or it may reverse. A disciplined analysis asks what has changed in fundamentals, valuations, market structure and risk. It also asks what evidence would challenge the thesis. A recent return chart is not a complete investment case.'},{heading:'Use a repeatable observation framework',body:'Choose a consistent universe and benchmark. Define the lookback periods used to measure relative performance. Compare sector participation with index breadth and trend structure. Record when leadership changes and whether the move is broad or concentrated. Include liquidity, valuation and company fundamentals before drawing conclusions. Keep the date and data source visible so that the observation can be reproduced.'},{heading:"A rotation signal needs more than a ranking table",body:"Suppose a sector moves to the top of a three-month performance ranking. Before interpreting that as a leadership change, compare it with longer lookbacks, its benchmark-relative performance and the number of constituents participating. Check whether the move is concentrated in one or two large companies. Then examine earnings revisions, valuations, liquidity and any event that may explain the change. A ranking table describes what happened over a chosen period. It does not explain why it happened or establish that it will continue. Record the observation date and methodology so the analysis can be repeated consistently."}],takeaways:['Market leadership changes as economic, financial and company conditions evolve.','A market-cycle framework organises evidence. It does not provide a timetable.','Index returns can conceal narrow participation.','Avoid chasing recent winners without examining valuation, fundamentals and risk.'],sources:[{label:'RBI: Monetary policy and macroeconomic publications',url:'https://www.rbi.org.in/'},{label:'NSE: Market statistics and indices',url:'https://www.nseindia.com/'},{label:'SEBI Investor: Understanding securities markets',url:'https://investor.sebi.gov.in/'}]},
'behavioural-biases-that-shape-investing':{category:'INVESTOR BEHAVIOUR',title:'The market is hard enough. Do not let familiar biases make it harder.',subtitle:'A practical guide to overconfidence, recency, loss aversion and the stories investors tell themselves.',read:'4 min read',tags:['Behavioural finance','Decision-making','Biases','Intermediate'],intro:'Investing is not only a problem of information. It is also a problem of interpretation. Two people can see the same price move and reach very different conclusions because of their experiences, incentives and emotions. Behavioural finance studies the predictable ways judgement can be distorted. Knowing the names of biases does not make anyone immune, but it can help create better decision processes.',sections:[{heading:'Recency bias: the latest period feels like the whole future',body:'After a strong rally, investors may assume recent returns will continue. After a sharp fall, they may expect losses to persist indefinitely. Recent information is vivid and easy to recall, but a short period may not represent the full range of possible outcomes. Use longer context, compare different market environments and separate a recent observation from a durable change in fundamentals.',visual:{type:'compare',title:'A useful distinction',items:['Observation: this asset has risen sharply recently','Assumption: it will keep rising at the same pace','Better question: what evidence supports or challenges that expectation?'],note:'A recent trend is information, not a forecast.'}},{heading:'Loss aversion: losses can feel larger than equivalent gains',body:'A decline can create a strong urge to avoid further discomfort, even when the original goal and time horizon have not changed. The opposite can also happen: an investor may hold a deteriorating position simply to avoid recognising a loss. A decision should be based on the investment facts and the investor’s circumstances, not on the purchase price alone.'},{heading:'Overconfidence: a good outcome can be mistaken for skill',body:'A profitable trade or successful fund choice can make an investor overestimate their ability to predict markets. A result may have been influenced by luck, broad market direction or risks that were not visible at the time. Keep a record of the thesis, evidence, risk and alternative explanations. Evaluate the process across many decisions rather than judging it by one outcome.'},{heading:'Confirmation bias: searching for support instead of truth',body:'Once an investor forms a view, it becomes tempting to notice supportive headlines and dismiss contradictory evidence. A simple countermeasure is to write down what would prove the thesis wrong before acting. Seek the strongest opposing argument, check primary sources and distinguish facts from interpretation. A good process should be able to change its mind.'},{heading:'Create friction before consequential decisions',body:'Use a written checklist for large decisions. Define the goal, time horizon, amount at risk, key assumptions, downside scenario and evidence that would change your view. Avoid making major changes immediately after an emotional market event. If a decision is urgent, identify why it cannot wait and verify the facts. Process cannot remove emotion, but it can stop emotion from being the only input.'},{heading:"Build a decision note before the next emotional moment",body:"Before making a consequential investment decision, write five short lines: the goal, the reason for the decision, the strongest evidence supporting it, the strongest evidence against it and the event that would make you reconsider. Add the amount at risk and the time horizon. Revisit the note after a meaningful period, not only when the price moves sharply. This creates a record that can reveal whether decisions are being driven by repeatable evidence or by the latest headline. It will not remove bias, but it makes the reasoning visible and easier to challenge."}],takeaways:['Recent performance is not a reliable forecast of future performance.','Avoid decisions driven only by the desire to realise gains or avoid losses.','Record the thesis and the evidence that would invalidate it.','Seek disconfirming information and use a checklist for consequential decisions.'],sources:[{label:'SEBI Investor: Investor awareness resources',url:'https://investor.sebi.gov.in/'},{label:'CFA Institute: Behavioural finance resources',url:'https://www.cfainstitute.org/en/research/foundation/2020/behavioral-finance'},{label:'Zerodha Varsity: Trading psychology',url:'https://zerodha.com/varsity/'}]},
'goal-based-investing-start-with-the-number':{category:'GOAL-BASED PLANNING',title:'Start with the goal, not the fund. Turn a vague wish into a plan.',subtitle:'A practical way to define the amount, date, flexibility and funding gap behind a financial goal.',read:'4 min read',tags:['Goal planning','Inflation','Cash flow','Beginner'],intro:'“I want to build wealth” is a meaningful aspiration, but it is difficult to translate into a financial plan. A goal becomes more useful when it has an amount, a date, a purpose and a clear view of how flexible it is. That information helps frame the investment horizon and the risks that may be appropriate to examine.',sections:[{heading:'Give the goal a name and a date',body:'Write down what the money is for and when it may be needed. Separate essential goals from optional ones. Record whether the date can move, whether the amount can be adjusted and what happens if the goal is only partly funded. A home deposit, education expense and retirement income need different cash-flow and risk considerations.',visual:{type:'flow',title:'Turn a wish into a defined goal',items:['Purpose and priority','Target date and flexibility','Estimated cost and inflation','Existing savings and future contributions'],note:'A clearer goal makes the planning assumptions easier to examine.'}},{heading:'Estimate the future cost, not just today’s price',body:'For goals several years away, the current price may understate the amount eventually required. Inflation differs across goods and services, and personal costs may not track a broad index exactly. Use an explicit assumption, show a range and revisit it periodically. Avoid false precision: a projection is a planning estimate, not a promise about future prices.'},{heading:'Map what is already available',body:'List earmarked savings, existing investments, expected contributions and any reliable future inflows. Separate assets that are genuinely available for the goal from money needed for emergencies or other commitments. Consider taxes, transaction costs and liquidity when estimating the amount that could actually be used. Do not count uncertain bonuses or asset sales as guaranteed funding.'},{heading:'Test the contribution required under different assumptions',body:'A planning calculator can help estimate the contribution needed to reach a target. Test several return assumptions and include a lower-return scenario. Market-linked returns are uncertain and arrive unevenly, so a single assumed rate can create a misleading sense of certainty. If the plan only works under an optimistic case, consider changing the amount, date, contribution or goal priority.'},{heading:'Give the plan a review trigger',body:'Review when income, family responsibilities, liabilities, goal cost or timing changes materially. A review can also be scheduled at sensible intervals. Avoid changing the investment approach just because another product has recently performed better. The goal is to keep the funding plan connected to real life, not to chase the latest return chart.'},{heading:"A worked inflation example",body:"Imagine an education expense costs ₹20 lakh today and the goal is eight years away. At an illustrative 6% annual inflation assumption, the estimated future cost is about ₹31.9 lakh. This is not a forecast of education inflation. Actual costs may rise faster or slower, and the estimate should be updated as better information becomes available. The example shows why a goal amount should not simply be copied from today’s price list. Once the future target is estimated, compare it with earmarked savings and affordable future contributions. Then test the funding plan under more than one investment-return assumption."}],takeaways:['Define each goal by purpose, amount, date and flexibility.','Account for inflation when estimating future costs.','Separate earmarked assets from emergency reserves and other commitments.','Use multiple return assumptions and revisit the plan when circumstances change.'],sources:[{label:'SEBI Investor: Financial planning and investor education',url:'https://investor.sebi.gov.in/'},{label:'RBI: Inflation and consumer price information',url:'https://www.rbi.org.in/'},{label:'AMFI Investor: Mutual fund investor education',url:'https://www.amfiindia.com/investor'}]}

};
function Visual({visual}:{visual:NonNullable<Section['visual']>}){return <figure className={'learning-visual '+visual.type}><figcaption>{visual.title}</figcaption><div className="learning-visual-items">{visual.items.map((item,i)=><div className="learning-visual-item" key={item}><span>{String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')}</span><strong>{item}</strong></div>)}</div><small>{visual.note}</small></figure>}
export function generateStaticParams(){return Object.keys(articles).map(slug=>({slug}));}
export async function generateMetadata({params}:{params:Promise<{slug:string}>}){const {slug}=await params;const a=articles[slug];if(!a)return {};const url=`https://shubham404-error.github.io/CapitalSense-portfolio-/insights/${slug}/`;return {title:a.title,description:a.subtitle,alternates:{canonical:url},openGraph:{title:a.title,description:a.subtitle,url,type:'article',siteName:'CapitalSense'}};}
export default async function Article({params}:{params:Promise<{slug:string}>}){const {slug}=await params;const a=articles[slug];if(!a)notFound();return <main className="inner-page"><article className="container article-detail"><Link className="text-link" href="/insights">← All insights</Link><span className="eyebrow">{a.category} · {a.read}</span><h1>{a.title}</h1><p className="article-subtitle" data-reveal="up">{a.subtitle}</p><p className="page-lead" data-reveal="up">{a.intro}</p><div className="article-tags" data-reveal="up">{a.tags.map(t=><span key={t}>{t}</span>)}</div><p className="article-disclaimer">For general investor education only. This article is not personalised investment advice or a recommendation to buy, sell or hold any security or mutual fund scheme. Mutual fund investments are subject to market risks. Read all scheme related documents carefully.</p>{a.sections.map((s,i)=><section className="article-section" data-reveal="up" key={s.heading}><span className="article-section-number">0{i+1}</span><h2>{s.heading}</h2><p>{s.body}</p>{s.visual&&<Visual visual={s.visual}/>}</section>)}<section className="article-takeaways" data-reveal="up"><h2>Keep these in mind</h2><ul>{a.takeaways.map(t=><li key={t}>{t}</li>)}</ul></section><section className="article-sources" data-reveal="up"><h2>Go a little deeper</h2><p>Further reading from educational and primary sources. CapitalSense is not affiliated with these publishers.</p><ul>{a.sources.map(s=><li key={s.url}><a href={s.url} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">{s.label} ↗</a></li>)}</ul></section><div className="inner-block"><h2>More thoughtful investing starts with better questions.</h2><Link className="text-link" href="/insights">Explore more articles ↗</Link></div></article></main>}